--r-- 1 agent agent    6154 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    5802 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   69475 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   22772 Nov 11 08:40 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     280 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    4008 Nov 11 08:44 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   10632 Nov 11 09:08 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   10632 Nov 11 09:06 references.pre_postdraft.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   27847 Nov 11 09:06 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   42189 Nov 11 09:08 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov 11 08:40 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:05 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:05 scripts\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:04 source_evidence\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 08:46 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     272 Nov 11 08:46 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov 11 08:40 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, double-blind)\n\n**Provisional title:** Verified Minimal-Investment Planning for Leaderboard Rank Targeting under Multiple Aggregation Rules\n\n## Central question\nUnder a declared per-task score-gain investment model, can a plan-verified planner find investments that make a target model strictly first on public multi-task leaderboards at a cost matching an evaluator-owned reference solver, across several aggregation rules, and how does it compare with naive baselines?\n\n## Central thesis\nThe sealed final evaluation (Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001/9002) is **qualified** (status completed, correct = true). Main efficiency relative to the reference solver is 1.0032 vs 0.8166 for the better naive baseline; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0. The claim is narrow and conditional on the investment model: planner cost-efficiency, not real training effects, not generalization to other boards, and not a ranking of aggregation rules.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Grounded artifacts hold final metrics, 2 final seeds, and 3 scored search attempts on HELM MMLU v1.13.0 (0 unscored). No `downstream_study` block exists.\n- **No per-rule or per-target cost data are retained.** The brief's question (how required investment varies with rule) therefore stays a motivating hypothesis, explicitly untested here. Missing diagnostics are unknown, not negative.\n- Efficiency > 1 means verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference (per-episode cap 2). Neither the reference nor the planner is claimed optimal.\n- Prior paper: none (not edit mode); status \"rejected\" is vacuous, the draft is fresh.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Proposed: verified minimal-investment planner (frozen search winner: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality; verified heuristics/MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score; boundary polishing; fallback).\n2. Baseline: naive uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost (better per case); only final main metric retained (0.8166).\n3. Supporting: reference solver defining the efficiency scale.\n4. Ablation/control: permuted-input and shuffled-null controls (final 1.0002; search 0.9967).\n5. Ablation: three search-stage variants on HELM (main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339). MILP layer: no measured difference; polishing: +0.0016 combined, 2 seeds, no significance claim.\n\n## Results focus\nFinal comparison with per-seed values; search-stage component comparison kept separate; descriptive search-to-final observation (1.0051 vs 1.0032, different data); explicit scope statement on what is not measured. Only uncertainty available: two seeds.\n\n## Figures (single-column template, text width ≈ 5.5 in)\n1. `overview_schematic` (required, full width, page 1-2, generate-concept-figure): matrix → investment model → rules → planner → verifier → efficiency vs reference.\n2. `final_efficiency_comparison` (required, matplotlib, full width at 0.8): planner vs baseline vs reference line; control and validity; ≤2 panels, ≤3 categories.\n3. `search_variant_comparison` (optional, matplotlib, 0.8 width): zoomed bars or a table for the three variants.\nPDF + 300 DPI PNG exported from source; LaTeX references PDF.\n\n## Page budget\nFill 8-9 pages with problem formalization and investment-model justification, rule set (conceptual sensitivity marked as not measured), planner/verification design, evaluation protocol and qualification rule, results, and a candid limitations section (no spillover, uniform costs, field K, cap policy, 2 seeds, unverified candidate diagnostics, no per-rule evidence). If content is thin, shorten claims; no filler.\n\n## Supplementary plan\n(1) Investment model, field, targets, rule definitions; (2) efficiency metric, evaluator protocol, full metric/per-seed tables; (3) planner components and verification procedure; (4) data provenance, snapshot SHAs, seeds, runtime, license caveats, LLM usage.\n\n## Warnings for writer\n- `references.bib` has only three on-topic entries (Dey 2014, Yang 2014, Obraztsova 2011); the rest are off-topic robust-statistics items. Starter literature is absent from the bib: cite only after verified addition.\n- Avoid chronology headings, pipeline-native labels (`combined_score`, \"correctness rate\", \"eligible\"), and any state-of-the-art, optimality or significance claims.\n- Anonymize; include LLM Usage section.\n\n## Forbidden headings/phrases\nExperiment 1/2, Search Progression, Milestones, First Valid Result, Campaign Phases, Iteration History; \"correctness rate\", \"correct and eligible\", \"combined_score\", \"rubric\", \"state of the art\", \"optimal planner\".\nfigures:\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.pdf\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.pdf.prov.json\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.png\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.png.prov.json\noverview_schematic.png\noverview_schematic.prompts.json\nsearch_variant_comparison.pdf\nsearch_variant_comparison.pdf.prov.json\nsearch_variant_comparison.png\nsearch_variant_comparison.png.prov.json\n\nglobal_memory:\n_idea_generation\nattempts_history.jsonl\nbest_program_manifest.json\nconclusions.md\nconclusions_history.jsonl\nconclusions_other_workers.md\nevents.jsonl\nidea_generation_failures.jsonl\nideas\nideas_history.jsonl\nlatest_run_manifest.json\nmemory.jsonl\nmemory.md\nouter_state.json\ntask\n\nscientific-evidence:\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ebd735c-ffc1-4510-b0aa-9e4a095cb3e8","uuid":"e808e88d-0869-4f68-9fd1-1e540572c535","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:08:38.763Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 3772\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:08 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:40 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     751 Nov 11 08:40 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:40 .claude\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 08:40 .paper_two_stage_enabled\nprw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 09:08 .postdraft_citation_weave_stream_20261111_084048.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3685 Nov 11 09:08 VENUE.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    3191 Nov 11 09:04 claims_grounding.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    3014 Nov 11 09:06 draft_reference_gaps.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    2300 Nov 11 09:06 draft_reference_gaps.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  146905 Nov 11 09:06 draft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3229 Nov 11 09:05 draft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 09:06 draft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     280 Nov 11 09:06 draft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    2017 Nov 11 09:05 figure_manifest.json\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:04 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      1