{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011k888YPHq8KqFXazNhrFGV","type":"tool_result","content":"42:Multi-task benchmarks are the standard instrument for comparing machine-learning models: a leaderboard collects scores of many models on many sub-datasets and an aggregation rule, most often the arithmetic mean, converts each model's score vector into a rank \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them. One class of actions is to include data related to some of the sub-datasets in a model's training, which raises its scores on those tasks. Prior work documents that leaderboards are sensitive to task selection \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, to aggregation choices \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}, to private testing and uneven sampling \\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and to benchmark leakage \\citep{Zhou2023DontMY,Sainz2023NLPEI,Yang2023RethinkingBA}. What remains less examined is a quantitative, rule-by-rule account of how large a targeted investment must be for a specific model to reach first place.\n64:\\paragraph{Multi-task benchmarks and their aggregation.} Broad suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench and its hard subset, HELM and SuperGLUE summarize models by averaging over many sub-tasks \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Srivastava2022BeyondTI,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks \\citep{Zhou2023InstructionFollowingEF,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Hendrycks2021MeasuringMP,Rein2023GPQAAG,Sprague2023MuSRTT,Wang2024MMLUProAM}. Critiques show that the mean is a modeling choice with consequences: task choice can reorder methods \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, leaderboard design embeds assumptions about user utility \\citep{Ethayarajh2020UtilityII}, and aggregate statistics need uncertainty-aware treatment \\citep{Agarwal2021DeepRL}. Social-choice formulations replace the mean with voting rules for models, agents or systems \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}. We use that rule family not to recommend a rule but as the set of aggregators over which a targeted-investment cost is defined.\n66:\\paragraph{Leaderboard gaming and contamination.} Evidence of strategic behavior includes selective private testing and uneven data sampling on the Chatbot Arena platform \\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}, as analyzed by \\citet{Singh2025TheLI}, training on evaluation data \\citep{Zhou2023DontMY}, per-benchmark contamination measurement \\citep{Sainz2023NLPEI}, and paraphrased test items that evade decontamination \\citep{Yang2023RethinkingBA}. Adaptive reuse of a holdout is a related validity threat \\citep{Dwork2015TheRH}. A game-theoretic treatment of benchmark designers and developers who post-train strategically is given by \\citet{Chen2026LeaderboardIM}. Our setting is complementary and simpler: a single actor, a fixed public matrix, and a computed cost of a hypothetical intervention, with no equilibrium analysis and no model of how training data transfers across tasks.\n68:\\paragraph{Computational social choice.} Manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems ask how little must change for a chosen candidate to win under a voting rule \\citep{Bartholdi1989TheCD,Faliszewski2006HowHI,Xia2012ComputingTM,Conitzer2016BarriersTM}. Complexity results depend on the rule, on the number of candidates, and on tie-breaking \\citep{Obraztsova2011TiesMC,Yang2014ElectionAW,Dey2014KernelizationCO}. Our problem is a continuous variant: the ``voters'' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores (not preferences directly), and cost is measured in score points or in touched tasks. We borrow the strict-win convention, under which ties count against the target.\n206:\\paragraph{Investment model.} Independent per-task gains without spillover contradict how training data usually behaves: data for one task often helps related ones, a contamination effect discussed in \\citet{Zhou2023DontMY} and \\citet{Sainz2023NLPEI}. Uniform costs, whether per point or per task, ignore that some tasks are cheaper to improve. The cap policy (100 for L1, field-best for L0) and the field definition (top 200 by mean, targets at ranks 2 and 10) are choices, and sensitivity to them was not measured. Treating a target as fixed also ignores that rivals could respond, which is the setting of \\citet{Chen2026LeaderboardIM}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ebd735c-ffc1-4510-b0aa-9e4a095cb3e8","uuid":"05749a56-fd92-48e5-80ef-b95412fc98af","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:08:51.020Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"42:Multi-task benchmarks are the standard instrument for comparing machine-learning models: a leaderboard collects scores of many models on many sub-datasets and an aggregation rule, most often the arithmetic mean, converts each model's score vector into a rank \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them. One class of actions is to include data related to some of the sub-datasets in a model's training, which raises its scores on those tasks. Prior work documents that leaderboards are sensitive to task selection \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, to aggregation choices \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}, to private testing and uneven sampling \\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and to benchmark leakage \\citep{Zhou2023DontMY,Sainz2023NLPEI,Yang2023RethinkingBA}. What remains less examined is a quantitative, rule-by-rule account of how large a targeted investment must be for a specific model to reach first place.\n64:\\paragraph{Multi-task benchmarks and their aggregation.} Broad suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench and its hard subset, HELM and SuperGLUE summarize models by averaging over many sub-tasks \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Srivastava2022BeyondTI,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks \\citep{Zhou2023InstructionFollowingEF,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Hendrycks2021MeasuringMP,Rein2023GPQAAG,Sprague2023MuSRTT,Wang2024MMLUProAM}. Critiques show that the mean is a modeling choice with consequences: task choice can reorder methods \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, leaderboard design embeds assumptions about user utility \\citep{Ethayarajh2020UtilityII}, and aggregate statistics need uncertainty-aware treatment \\citep{Agarwal2021DeepRL}. Social-choice formulations replace the mean with voting rules for models, agents or systems \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}. We use that rule family not to recommend a rule but as the set of aggregators over which a targeted-investment cost is defined.\n66:\\paragraph{Leaderboard gaming and contamination.} Evidence of strategic behavior includes selective private testing and uneven data sampling on the Chatbot Arena platform \\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}, as analyzed by \\citet{Singh2025TheLI}, training on evaluation data \\citep{Zhou2023DontMY}, per-benchmark contamination measurement \\citep{Sainz2023NLPEI}, and paraphrased test items that evade decontamination \\citep{Yang2023RethinkingBA}. Adaptive reuse of a holdout is a related validity threat \\citep{Dwork2015TheRH}. A game-theoretic treatment of benchmark designers and developers who post-train strategically is given by \\citet{Chen2026LeaderboardIM}. Our setting is complementary and simpler: a single actor, a fixed public matrix, and a computed cost of a hypothetical intervention, with no equilibrium analysis and no model of how training data transfers across tasks.\n68:\\paragraph{Computational social choice.} Manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems ask how little must change for a chosen candidate to win under a voting rule \\citep{Bartholdi1989TheCD,Faliszewski2006HowHI,Xia2012ComputingTM,Conitzer2016BarriersTM}. Complexity results depend on the rule, on the number of candidates, and on tie-breaking \\citep{Obraztsova2011TiesMC,Yang2014ElectionAW,Dey2014KernelizationCO}. Our problem is a continuous variant: the ``voters'' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores (not preferences directly), and cost is measured in score points or in touched tasks. We borrow the strict-win convention, under which ties count against the target.\n206:\\paragraph{Investment model.} Independent per-task gains without spillover contradict how training data usually behaves: data for one task often helps related ones, a contamination effect discussed in \\citet{Zhou2023DontMY} and \\citet{Sainz2023NLPEI}. Uniform costs, whether per point or per task, ignore that some tasks are cheaper to improve. The cap policy (100 for L1, field-best for L0) and the field definition (top 200 by mean, targets at ranks 2 and 10) are choices, and sensitivity to them was not measured. Treating a target as fixed also ignores that rivals could respond, which is the setting of \\citet{Chen2026LeaderboardIM}.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
