{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018xMrL19PTRjNbsWpG4GFp9","type":"tool_result","content":"claims_grounding.json:106:    \"Yang2014ElectionAW\",\nclaims_grounding.json:107:    \"Dey2014KernelizationCO\"\npaper.tex:42:Multi-task benchmarks are the standard instrument for comparing machine-learning models: a leaderboard collects scores of many models on many sub-datasets and an aggregation rule, most often the arithmetic mean, converts each model's score vector into a rank \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, \npaper.tex:64:\\paragraph{Multi-task benchmarks and their aggregation.} Broad suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench and its hard subset, HELM and SuperGLUE summarize models by averaging over many sub-tasks \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Srivastava2022BeyondTI,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks \\citep{Zhou2023InstructionFollo\npaper.tex:68:\\paragraph{Computational social choice.} Manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems ask how little must change for a chosen candidate to win under a voting rule \\citep{Bartholdi1989TheCD,Faliszewski2006HowHI,Xia2012ComputingTM,Conitzer2016BarriersTM}. Complexity results depend on the rule, on the number of candidates, and on tie-breaking \\citep{Obraztsova2011TiesMC,Yang2014E\n38:Multi-task leaderboards turn a matrix of per-task scores into a single ranking through an aggregation rule, and a developer who can raise a target model's score on selected tasks, for example by tr\n52:\\item A held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards against a reference solver, naive baselines and two controls, with a separate development comparison of three planner configurations o\n64:\\paragraph{Multi-task benchmarks and their aggregation.} Broad suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench and its hard subset, HELM and SuperGLUE summarize models by averaging over many sub-tasks \\citep{Hendry\n136:\\paragraph{Data and splits.} Development uses the 57 HELM MMLU v1.13.0 subject columns as tasks. The held-out evaluation uses two boards built from the Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots: a six-benchm\n146:\\caption{Held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards (two evaluator seeds). (a) Main efficiency of the verified planner and of the better of two naive baselines per case, against the re\n219:We formalized targeted investment on multi-task leaderboards and evaluated a verification-first planner that returns only plans re-checked against an exact replica of the aggregation rule. On two \n248:\\caption{Held-out metrics from the sealed final evaluation (Open LLM Leaderboard boards).}\n287:\\paragraph{Data.} Development: HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 (summary date 2025-01-10), 57 subject files, exact-match column, each file pinned by SHA-256 in the task manifest. Held-out: Open LLM Leade\n{\n \"citation_count\": 26,\n \"citation_key\": \"Dey2014KernelizationCO\",\n \"id\": \"ref.s2.780d2c0f14c6b2f001d173b85b36c9b2052496b6\",\n \"paper_id\": \"780d2c0f14c6b2f001d173b85b36c9b2052496b6\",\n \"query\": \"manipulation of Borda and Copeland rules complexity\",\n \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-11T08:41:42.894912+00:00\",\n \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/780d2c0f14c6b2f001d173b85b36c9b2052496b6?fields=paperId%2Ctitle%2Cyear%2Cvenue%2CcitationCount%2CcitationStyles%2Curl\",\n \"title\": \"Kernelization complexity of possible winner and coalitional manipulation problems in voting\",\n \"venue\": \"Theoretical Computer Science\",\n \"year\": 2014\n}\ndict_keys(['citation_count', 'citation_key', 'id', 'paper_id', 'query', 'retrieved_at', 'source_url', 'title', 'venue', 'year'])","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ebd735c-ffc1-4510-b0aa-9e4a095cb3e8","uuid":"3a48f1e0-40cd-4d13-9a40-27ae98bb20e5","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:12:37.018Z","tool_use_result":